WHAT WE WANT FROM YOU

The system hands you one candidate at a time. Your job is to turn it into a working
instance, stage by stage, or to find out that it cannot be made to work and drop it with the
reason. Then the system hands you the next candidate. You do not choose candidates and you do
not report results; the system keeps the record.

A candidate comes with these fields, as the search recorded them: {{ instance.identity_fields | join(', ') }}.
Any other field on the candidate line is a note the search step made about it (a commit, a
file, a method it found; a name it guessed): a lead to start from and check, not a fact.
A commit the search recorded was in the repository when it looked. Get it by hash into an
empty repository: `git init repo; git -C repo fetch --depth 1 <url> <sha>; git -C repo
checkout <sha>`. A shallow clone of a branch holds only that branch's newest commits, and
`git ls-remote` lists branch and tag tips only: neither of them missing the commit shows
that it is gone. The commit is gone only when the fetch by hash fails. A shallow fetch also
hides parents: `%P` prints nothing and `<sha>^` is unknown until you deepen (`--depth 2`, or
`git fetch --deepen=1 <url>`). A commit has no parent only when a deepened fetch shows none.
It has no tags and no history either: a build that runs `git describe` or a version script
needs `git fetch --tags <url>` or `git fetch --unshallow <url>` first.
{% if instance.fields_to_establish %}Its instance.json also carries {{ instance.fields_to_establish | join(', ') }}. Those
are NOT given to you: establishing them, by finding in the candidate what the definition of
an instance above describes, is part of your work in the stages. A candidate is dropped for
lacking them only after you have looked. When the search's note for one of these fields
names something that cannot be used here (a tool that needs a server, a version that no
longer exists, a name that does not resolve), the note is wrong, not the candidate:
establish the field with what works from source alone, and record what you chose.
{% if instance.identity_is_repository_only %}The same when a noted commit, file or method turns out not to be what the definition
describes (a method that was added rather than changed, a commit that touched only tests):
that note is one lead that did not check out, and the candidate is the REPOSITORY. Look in
its history for what the definition describes before you drop it, and drop only when you
found none, saying what you looked at.
{% else %}You WILL read other commits to establish them, and you are meant to: the version before a fix
is its parent, the version a comment was corrected in is a later commit, and you reach those
by deepening the clone and reading what is there. Establishing a field from the history is
the work.

What does not change is the identity: {{ instance.identity_fields | join(', ') }}. Those name
THIS candidate. Read around them as much as the definition needs, but never REPLACE one. If
what they name turns out not to be what the definition describes, this candidate is dropped
with that reason -- do not go hunting the repository for a better commit to put in its place.
A different commit is a DIFFERENT candidate, and delivering it would file an instance under an
identity that no longer describes what is inside it.
{% endif %}{% endif %}

WHAT AN INSTANCE IS

A directory of files, bench/, that builds and runs the program on a blank machine:
  Dockerfile      installs the tools the program needs: the compiler, the libraries. It is
                  built on its own, with no files beside it: no COPY or ADD of your scripts
                  (they are placed at /bench after the container starts)
  setup.sh        fetches the code at the exact commit into /work/repo
  instance.json   the candidate's identity, the fields you were given
  a few scripts   this benchmark's scripts, one job each; each stage names the ones to write
Everything the instance needs is in these files. Anything that exists only on your machine
and not in them does not count.
